1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:02,521
Imagine someone could control your

2
00:00:02,535 --> 00:00:04,475
decisions without touching you,

3
00:00:04,505 --> 00:00:05,426
threatening you,

4
00:00:05,456 --> 00:00:07,238
or even speaking to you.

5
00:00:07,419 --> 00:00:08,318
They could make you buy

6
00:00:08,331 --> 00:00:09,759
things you don't need,

7
00:00:09,789 --> 00:00:11,608
believe things that aren't true,

8
00:00:11,638 --> 00:00:13,879
and even doubt your own memories.

9
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:15,878
And the scariest part?

10
00:00:16,374 --> 00:00:17,554
You might think all those

11
00:00:17,568 --> 00:00:19,363
decisions were yours.

12
00:00:19,816 --> 00:00:21,185
Welcome to the hidden world

13
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:23,554
of psychological manipulation.

14
00:00:27,289 --> 00:00:29,349
Let's try a little experiment.

15
00:00:29,379 --> 00:00:30,004
Imagine you're

16
00:00:30,016 --> 00:00:31,504
walking into a room.

17
00:00:31,534 --> 00:00:33,447
There are ten people inside.

18
00:00:33,477 --> 00:00:34,489
They all point towards a

19
00:00:34,502 --> 00:00:36,184
completely ordinary object

20
00:00:36,214 --> 00:00:37,314
and confidently tell you

21
00:00:37,327 --> 00:00:38,739
it's something different.

22
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:40,253
You know they're wrong.

23
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:40,991
But suddenly,

24
00:00:41,021 --> 00:00:42,946
you start questioning yourself.

25
00:00:43,096 --> 00:00:44,046
Why?

26
00:00:44,395 --> 00:00:45,483
Because your brain doesn't

27
00:00:45,495 --> 00:00:46,880
just want to be correct.

28
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:48,872
It also wants to belong.

29
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,063
And that's where the first

30
00:00:50,075 --> 00:00:52,288
psychological trick begins.

31
00:00:54,524 --> 00:00:56,587
Humans are social creatures.

32
00:00:56,617 --> 00:00:57,825
We naturally observe

33
00:00:57,855 --> 00:00:59,067
other people to understand

34
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:00,675
how we should behave.

35
00:01:00,705 --> 00:01:01,580
In the famous Asch

36
00:01:01,592 --> 00:01:02,987
conformity experiments,

37
00:01:03,017 --> 00:01:04,500
participants sometimes gave

38
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:05,792
incorrect answers about the

39
00:01:05,805 --> 00:01:07,317
lengths of lines simply because

40
00:01:07,330 --> 00:01:08,780
other people in the group had

41
00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:10,867
already given those answers.

42
00:01:10,998 --> 00:01:12,462
Think about that.

43
00:01:12,604 --> 00:01:13,604
The correct answer was

44
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:15,010
right in front of them.

45
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,127
Yet social pressure

46
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:18,240
influenced their responses.

47
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:20,298
This is called conformity.

48
00:01:20,643 --> 00:01:22,509
And it happens in everyday life.

49
00:01:22,539 --> 00:01:23,822
Fashion trends.

50
00:01:23,852 --> 00:01:25,634
Social media opinions.

51
00:01:25,664 --> 00:01:27,327
Popular beliefs.

52
00:01:27,441 --> 00:01:28,349
Sometimes,

53
00:01:28,379 --> 00:01:29,379
we don't follow something

54
00:01:29,391 --> 00:01:31,122
because we genuinely like it.

55
00:01:31,152 --> 00:01:32,090
We follow it because

56
00:01:32,102 --> 00:01:33,752
everyone else seems to.

57
00:01:36,045 --> 00:01:37,483
Now imagine hearing the same

58
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:39,298
statement every single day.

59
00:01:39,328 --> 00:01:41,606
At first, you don't believe it.

60
00:01:41,636 --> 00:01:42,498
But after hearing it

61
00:01:42,511 --> 00:01:43,643
hundreds of times,

62
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:45,509
it starts sounding familiar.

63
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:46,472
And strangely,

64
00:01:46,502 --> 00:01:47,677
familiar information can

65
00:01:47,689 --> 00:01:49,827
sometimes feel more believable.

66
00:01:49,971 --> 00:01:51,246
Psychologists call this

67
00:01:51,258 --> 00:01:53,171
the Illusory Truth Effect.

68
00:01:53,495 --> 00:01:54,870
It's one reason repetition

69
00:01:54,883 --> 00:01:56,920
is so powerful in advertising

70
00:01:56,933 --> 00:01:58,495
and misinformation.

71
00:01:58,611 --> 00:01:59,671
But remember:

72
00:01:59,701 --> 00:02:00,838
Hearing something a thousand

73
00:02:00,851 --> 00:02:02,617
times doesn't make it true.

74
00:02:02,647 --> 00:02:04,630
It only makes it familiar.

75
00:02:06,856 --> 00:02:07,906
Here's another way people

76
00:02:07,918 --> 00:02:09,734
influence your decisions.

77
00:02:09,764 --> 00:02:11,404
They don't attack your logic.

78
00:02:11,434 --> 00:02:13,422
They target your emotions.

79
00:02:13,665 --> 00:02:14,715
Imagine someone makes

80
00:02:14,727 --> 00:02:15,790
you feel guilty whenever

81
00:02:15,802 --> 00:02:17,302
you disagree with them.

82
00:02:17,347 --> 00:02:18,722
Or makes you afraid of losing

83
00:02:18,735 --> 00:02:20,585
them whenever you set a boundary.

84
00:02:20,701 --> 00:02:21,296
Eventually,

85
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:22,639
you might start doing things just

86
00:02:22,651 --> 00:02:25,214
to avoid feeling guilty or afraid.

87
00:02:25,344 --> 00:02:27,669
This is emotional manipulation.

88
00:02:27,885 --> 00:02:29,230
Fear, guilt,

89
00:02:29,260 --> 00:02:31,135
insecurity and even excitement can

90
00:02:31,148 --> 00:02:33,354
influence how we make decisions.

91
00:02:33,384 --> 00:02:34,634
And when someone understands

92
00:02:34,647 --> 00:02:36,229
which emotions affect you most,

93
00:02:36,259 --> 00:02:37,059
they may use those

94
00:02:37,072 --> 00:02:38,834
emotions to pressure you.

95
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:42,313
Now comes one of the most

96
00:02:42,326 --> 00:02:44,735
damaging forms of manipulation.

97
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:46,248
Gaslighting.

98
00:02:46,778 --> 00:02:48,344
Imagine someone repeatedly

99
00:02:48,357 --> 00:02:50,799
denies things they said or did.

100
00:02:50,829 --> 00:02:51,460
They tell you that

101
00:02:51,473 --> 00:02:52,167
you're remembering

102
00:02:52,197 --> 00:02:53,839
everything incorrectly.

103
00:02:53,869 --> 00:02:54,803
They make you question

104
00:02:54,817 --> 00:02:56,632
your own experiences.

105
00:02:56,662 --> 00:02:57,435
And slowly,

106
00:02:57,465 --> 00:02:58,452
you begin trusting their

107
00:02:58,465 --> 00:02:59,623
version of events more

108
00:02:59,636 --> 00:03:01,275
than your own memory.

109
00:03:01,461 --> 00:03:02,605
That's why gaslighting

110
00:03:02,619 --> 00:03:04,168
can be so harmful.

111
00:03:04,198 --> 00:03:06,261
It doesn't just create confusion.

112
00:03:06,291 --> 00:03:07,712
It can damage your confidence

113
00:03:07,726 --> 00:03:09,233
in your own judgment.

114
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:10,315
But remember,

115
00:03:10,345 --> 00:03:11,432
one disagreement or

116
00:03:11,445 --> 00:03:12,632
different memory isn't

117
00:03:12,645 --> 00:03:14,400
automatically gaslighting.

118
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:15,805
It's the repeated pattern of

119
00:03:15,817 --> 00:03:17,792
manipulation that matters.

120
00:03:18,315 --> 00:03:18,790
So,

121
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:19,818
can someone actually

122
00:03:19,831 --> 00:03:21,362
control your mind?

123
00:03:21,804 --> 00:03:23,638
Not like a remote control.

124
00:03:23,668 --> 00:03:24,631
But they can influence

125
00:03:24,643 --> 00:03:25,551
what you notice,

126
00:03:25,581 --> 00:03:26,663
what you believe,

127
00:03:26,693 --> 00:03:27,393
how you feel and

128
00:03:27,406 --> 00:03:29,318
sometimes what you decide.

129
00:03:29,434 --> 00:03:31,158
And here's the interesting part.

130
00:03:31,188 --> 00:03:32,438
You don't need a mysterious

131
00:03:32,451 --> 00:03:33,838
hypnotist or a master

132
00:03:33,851 --> 00:03:35,715
manipulator for this to happen.

133
00:03:35,745 --> 00:03:37,940
Advertising, social pressure,

134
00:03:37,970 --> 00:03:39,445
online trends and everyday

135
00:03:39,458 --> 00:03:40,545
relationships can all

136
00:03:40,570 --> 00:03:42,595
influence human behaviour.

137
00:03:42,715 --> 00:03:43,669
Sometimes,

138
00:03:43,699 --> 00:03:45,109
the strongest manipulation

139
00:03:45,122 --> 00:03:46,919
doesn't look like control.

140
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:49,088
It looks like your own choice.

141
00:03:51,225 --> 00:03:53,237
So how do you protect yourself?

142
00:03:53,293 --> 00:03:54,331
Pause before making

143
00:03:54,343 --> 00:03:56,031
emotional decisions.

144
00:03:56,061 --> 00:03:57,393
Question information,

145
00:03:57,423 --> 00:03:59,217
even when everyone agrees with it.

146
00:03:59,247 --> 00:04:00,660
Look for evidence instead of

147
00:04:00,672 --> 00:04:02,701
blindly trusting repetition.

148
00:04:02,731 --> 00:04:03,643
And never let someone

149
00:04:03,656 --> 00:04:04,656
make you feel guilty for

150
00:04:04,668 --> 00:04:06,318
thinking independently.

151
00:04:06,635 --> 00:04:07,885
Because the moment you stop

152
00:04:07,899 --> 00:04:09,842
questioning your decisions...

153
00:04:09,883 --> 00:04:11,067
Is the moment someone else

154
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:13,090
can start making them for you.

155
00:04:13,867 --> 00:04:15,522
This is FACTMAN.

156
00:04:15,596 --> 00:04:16,979
Stay curious.

157
00:04:17,009 --> 00:04:18,567
Question everything.
